{
  "dom_id": "step-q140545",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The rate at which the number of visitors at a particular festival changes is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=4t+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of people per hour, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nHow many thousands of people did the number of visitors increase by between hours $t=1$ and $t=3?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given a **rate of change** in the number of visitors $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of visitors between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4t+2)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(2{t}^{2}+2t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(3)^{2}+2(3))-(2(1)^{2}+2(1)) \\\\  & =18+6-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between hours $t=1$ and $t=3,$ the number of visitors increased by $20$ thousand people.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
